  },
  {
    background: 'Data science or statistics background',
    reality:
      'The highest-overlap case: many of the technical skills transfer directly into business analytics and product-management MBA tracks. The real question here is rarely "is my background wasted" — it is whether you need the MBA credential at all, since the market already pays for the technical skill alone.',
  },
  {
    background: 'Environmental science or earth sciences background',
    reality:
      'Smaller direct overlap with mainstream MBA tracks, but genuine value in sustainability, ESG, and energy-sector management roles, which are a growing niche most MBA cohorts do not have anyone credible to fill.',
  },
];

const checkpointItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Pull',
    body: 'When you picture the actual daily work five years from now, does it look like being the person who runs the experiment and defends the finding, or the person who decides what gets built, funded, or sold based on findings from other people?',
    note: 'Be honest about which version excites you on an ordinary Tuesday, not which one sounds better at a family function.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Evidence',
    body: 'Do you already have a signal that research suits you — a strong undergraduate project, a publication, a professor who has pushed you toward a PhD? Or is your MSc interest more about liking the subject in class than having tested the research grind itself?',
    note: 'Liking a subject and enjoying the slow, uncertain, often-failing process of research are two different things. Test the second one before betting years on it.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Money and timeline',
    body: 'Can you sustain a JRF stipend (commonly around Rs 37,000-42,000 a month) through a multi-year PhD, or do you need income to climb faster than a research track allows? Run this against your actual family and personal financial pressure, not an assumed future professor salary.',
