];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'MSc vs MBA India: which one should a science graduate choose?',
    a: 'It depends on whether your actual interest is in generating scientific findings or in deciding how those findings get funded, built, or sold. An MSc suits someone who wants to go deeper into one subject, is realistically preparing for CSIR-NET, GATE, or a similar exam, and can sustain a modest research stipend for a few years. An MBA suits someone who wants a faster, broader move into management, is drawn to cross-functional decision-making over narrow technical depth, and is willing to fund or take a loan for the program. Neither is a safer default — match the degree to what the actual daily work looks like, not to which one sounds more impressive.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does an MSc in Physics, Chemistry, or Biotechnology waste your science background if you do an MBA afterward?',
    a: 'No, and in fields like chemistry, biology, and biotechnology it is usually the opposite — an MBA with real subject depth behind it is genuinely rare and valuable in pharma, healthcare, agritech, and FMCG management, where most MBA graduates cannot credibly discuss the underlying science. The overlap is strongest for physics, maths, statistics, and data-science MSc backgrounds moving into analytics, quant, or strategy MBA tracks. The weaker overlap cases are narrower subjects like environmental science, though even those find a real niche in sustainability and ESG-focused management roles.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What can you do with an MSc in India besides a PhD?',
    a: 'MSc graduates in India commonly move into research assistant roles, quality control and quality assurance positions in pharma or manufacturing, clinical research and regulatory affairs, technical or scientific writing, data analyst roles (especially from statistics, data science, or maths-heavy MSc programs), teaching, and government research or PSU roles reached through a qualifying GATE score. A PhD is one strong path, not the only one — many MSc graduates build a full career in applied, industry-facing science roles without ever pursuing a doctorate.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is an MSc or an MBA better paid in India?',
    a: 'On average, a strong MBA from a well-ranked institute outpays a typical MSc, with strong-program MBA outcomes commonly landing in the Rs 10-25 lakh range and higher at the very top institutes, against a typical MSc starting range of roughly Rs 3-8 lakh. But this comparison is incomplete on its own: it ignores the two years of MBA tuition and lost income, ignores that MSc-linked government, PSU, and CSIR-NET-JRF-to-faculty paths carry long-term stability an MBA route does not guarantee, and ignores that field-specific MSc roles like data science can pay close to entry-level MBA-analytics pay without the MBA cost at all.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you do an MBA after MSc in India, and is CAT open to MSc graduates?',
    a: 'Yes. CAT, XAT, GMAT, and most other MBA entrance exams accept any postgraduate or graduate degree that meets the minimum marks criterion, so an MSc qualifies like any other bachelor\'s or master\'s degree would. Many science graduates specifically use CAT or XAT after an MSc, since MSc-trained quantitative reasoning is a genuine advantage in the exam\'s data-interpretation and quant sections, provided verbal ability and reading comprehension are prepared for separately rather than assumed to come naturally.',
  },
